[
  {
    "filename": "PPT_MMAT5390Lecture1_page_023.pdf",
    "page_index": 23,
    "core_content": {
      "parsing_res_list": [
        {
          "block_label": "paragraph_title",
          "block_content": "What is a digital image?",
          "block_bbox": [
            714,
            74,
            2132,
            203
          ],
          "block_id": 0,
          "block_order": 1
        },
        {
          "block_label": "paragraph_title",
          "block_content": "Mathematical definition:",
          "block_bbox": [
            70,
            324,
            1248,
            403
          ],
          "block_id": 1,
          "block_order": 2
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "A A2D (grayscale)digital image is a 2D function defined on a 2D domain (usually rectangular domain):",
          "block_bbox": [
            213,
            445,
            2824,
            614
          ],
          "block_id": 2,
          "block_order": 3
        },
        {
          "block_label": "formula",
          "block_content": "f:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb{R}",
          "block_bbox": [
            1135,
            671,
            1647,
            779
          ],
          "block_id": 3,
          "block_order": 4
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "$f(x,y)$ is called the brightness/intensity/grey level;",
          "block_bbox": [
            212,
            855,
            2171,
            939
          ],
          "block_id": 4,
          "block_order": 5
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "$(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})$ is the spatial coordinates image.",
          "block_bbox": [
            214,
            972,
            1884,
            1047
          ],
          "block_id": 5,
          "block_order": 6
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "Thus, a 2D digital image looks like this:",
          "block_bbox": [
            218,
            1078,
            1732,
            1155
          ],
          "block_id": 6,
          "block_order": 7
        },
        {
          "block_label": "formula",
          "block_content": "f(x,y)=\\left[\\begin{matrix}{f(1,1)}&{f(1,2)}&{\\dots}&{f(1,N)}\\\\ {f(2,1)}&{f(2,2)}&{\\dots}&{f(2,N)}\\\\ {\\vdots}&{\\vdots}&{}&{\\vdots}\\\\ {f(N,1)}&{f(N,2)}&{\\dots}&{f(N,N)}\\\\ \\end{matrix}\\right]",
          "block_bbox": [
            687,
            1199,
            2180,
            1623
          ],
          "block_id": 7,
          "block_order": 8
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "Each element in thematrix is called p pixel 1((picture element));",
          "block_bbox": [
            215,
            1727,
            2435,
            1802
          ],
          "block_id": 8,
          "block_order": 9
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "Usually,$0\\leq f(x,y)\\leq G-1\\quad{\\mathrm{\\boldmath~{\\sf~a n d}~}}\\left(N=2^{n},G=2^{m}\\right)$ ",
          "block_bbox": [
            214,
            1831,
            2119,
            1916
          ],
          "block_id": 9,
          "block_order": 10
        },
        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "IMAGER PROCESSING IS RELATED TO LINEAR ALGEBRA!!",
          "block_bbox": [
            327,
            2026,
            2675,
            2107
          ],
          "block_id": 10,
          "block_order": 11
        }
      ],
      "markdown": "## What is a digital image?\n\n## Mathematical definition:\n\nA A2D (grayscale)digital image is a 2D function defined on a 2D domain (usually rectangular domain):\n\n$$f:\\Omega\\to\\mathbb{R}$$\n\n$f(x,y)$ is called the brightness/intensity/grey level;\n\n$(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y})$ is the spatial coordinates image.\n\nThus, a 2D digital image looks like this:\n\n$$f(x,y)=\\left[\\begin{matrix}{f(1,1)}&{f(1,2)}&{\\dots}&{f(1,N)}\\\\ {f(2,1)}&{f(2,2)}&{\\dots}&{f(2,N)}\\\\ {\\vdots}&{\\vdots}&{}&{\\vdots}\\\\ {f(N,1)}&{f(N,2)}&{\\dots}&{f(N,N)}\\\\ \\end{matrix}\\right]$$\n\nEach element in thematrix is called p pixel 1((picture element));\n\nUsually,$0\\leq f(x,y)\\leq G-1\\quad{\\mathrm{\\boldmath~{\\sf~a n d}~}}\\left(N=2^{n},G=2^{m}\\right)$ \n\nIMAGER PROCESSING IS RELATED TO LINEAR ALGEBRA!!",
      "tables": [],
